WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.130 --> 00:00:01.380
- Your husband was on,

00:00:01.380 --> 00:00:03.410
and I gotta be honest with you,

00:00:03.410 --> 00:00:05.410
Tom might talk as much as I do,

00:00:05.410 --> 00:00:06.743
and that is a lot.
(Rita laughing)

00:00:06.743 --> 00:00:07.750
When we were interviewing,

00:00:07.750 --> 00:00:09.567
I was like, "I have met my match.

00:00:09.567 --> 00:00:11.640
"Challenge accepted, universe."

00:00:11.640 --> 00:00:12.473
- Throw it down.

00:00:12.473 --> 00:00:13.670
The gauntlet is down.

00:00:13.670 --> 00:00:15.427
- But you're chatty, too, though,

00:00:15.427 --> 00:00:16.680
but do you mind it?

00:00:16.680 --> 00:00:18.347
Or are you kind of like,
- No, no.

00:00:18.347 --> 00:00:19.410
"Whew, that's a lot."

00:00:19.410 --> 00:00:22.420
- Look, Tom and I, that
was one of the things

00:00:22.420 --> 00:00:25.320
that when we met, we
just got along instantly

00:00:25.320 --> 00:00:27.750
because first of all, I
love a good storyteller.

00:00:27.750 --> 00:00:30.960
So anybody who can tell a good
story, I'm down with that.

00:00:30.960 --> 00:00:32.980
And I don't know.

00:00:32.980 --> 00:00:35.140
My family was very vocal growing up,

00:00:35.140 --> 00:00:36.410
so we were--

00:00:36.410 --> 00:00:37.243
- Hence, the Greek.

00:00:37.243 --> 00:00:38.250
- Hence, the Greek thing,
(Kelly laughing)

00:00:38.250 --> 00:00:40.750
exactly, like talking in food.

00:00:40.750 --> 00:00:43.053
That was pretty much our whole existence.

00:00:44.080 --> 00:00:45.210
No, I love that.

00:00:45.210 --> 00:00:46.580
He makes me laugh all the time.

00:00:46.580 --> 00:00:47.600
I think that's--

00:00:47.600 --> 00:00:48.870
- He's a great storyteller.

00:00:48.870 --> 00:00:50.910
- He is a really good storyteller.

00:00:50.910 --> 00:00:51.930
- I was like this the whole time,

00:00:51.930 --> 00:00:53.280
and I was like, "Oh God,
I'm interviewing you."

00:00:53.280 --> 00:00:54.397
Yeah, I was like,
(Rita laughing)

00:00:54.397 --> 00:00:55.230
"Aah, I forgot."

00:00:55.230 --> 00:00:56.110
I was just listening.

00:00:56.110 --> 00:00:57.420
- We're gonna have you
guys over for dinner,

00:00:57.420 --> 00:00:58.510
and we can tell lots more stories.

00:00:58.510 --> 00:00:59.580
- Oh my god, it was great.

00:00:59.580 --> 00:01:01.210
I think the thing that's
great about both of you,

00:01:01.210 --> 00:01:02.920
meeting both of you backstage,

00:01:02.920 --> 00:01:04.040
you're super duper famous,

00:01:04.040 --> 00:01:05.880
but you're super duper chill,

00:01:05.880 --> 00:01:07.380
and that doesn't always happen.

00:01:07.380 --> 00:01:10.230
So congratulations on
being normal. (clapping)

00:01:10.230 --> 00:01:11.167
- Thank you!
(audience clapping)

00:01:11.167 --> 00:01:12.233
(audience cheering)

00:01:12.233 --> 00:01:13.580
- That doesn't happen all the time.

00:01:13.580 --> 00:01:15.760
- But I think the same thing about you.

00:01:15.760 --> 00:01:17.227
I was asking you backstage,

00:01:17.227 --> 00:01:18.680
"How do you do everything that you do?"

00:01:18.680 --> 00:01:19.680
I'm completely

00:01:19.680 --> 00:01:20.610
mesmerized by it.
- You're like me though.

00:01:20.610 --> 00:01:22.620
I just listed a thousand jobs you have.

00:01:22.620 --> 00:01:23.740
(laughing)

00:01:23.740 --> 00:01:25.120
I was like, "What?"

00:01:25.120 --> 00:01:26.130
We have found ourself.

00:01:26.130 --> 00:01:27.950
We have found each other.

00:01:27.950 --> 00:01:29.590
- But you know what, I love it.

00:01:29.590 --> 00:01:30.940
I love doing what I do.

00:01:30.940 --> 00:01:31.890
I feel really blessed

00:01:31.890 --> 00:01:33.380
to be able to do it,
- [Kelly] Amen.

00:01:33.380 --> 00:01:36.240
- and as I continue to do it...

00:01:36.240 --> 00:01:39.300
I've been working since I was
14 years old in this business,

00:01:39.300 --> 00:01:40.440
- and I had a job here--
- [Kelly] Wow!

00:01:40.440 --> 00:01:44.030
at this studio when I was a teenager

00:01:44.030 --> 00:01:46.780
at this music concert venue

00:01:46.780 --> 00:01:48.040
called the Universal Amphitheatre.

00:01:48.040 --> 00:01:50.090
Now it's the Harry Potter ride.

00:01:50.090 --> 00:01:50.950
Have you been on it?
(audience laughing)

00:01:50.950 --> 00:01:53.810
People used to sing there.
- Yes, I have been on it.

00:01:53.810 --> 00:01:55.740
I did get very nauseous.
(Rita laughing)

00:01:55.740 --> 00:01:56.703
I'm not into simulation.

00:01:56.703 --> 00:01:57.580
- No, no, no.

00:01:57.580 --> 00:01:58.413
It's hard.
- I was like, "Oh my god,

00:01:58.413 --> 00:01:59.916
"the Dementors are going to kill me."

00:01:59.916 --> 00:02:00.930
(laughing)

00:02:00.930 --> 00:02:02.750
So what do y'all do, though,
to get away from it all?

00:02:02.750 --> 00:02:03.700
Do you do theme parks

00:02:03.700 --> 00:02:04.690
like that, or do you--

00:02:04.690 --> 00:02:06.210
- Yeah, we do do theme parks,

00:02:06.210 --> 00:02:08.430
but one of the things that we like to do

00:02:08.430 --> 00:02:09.950
and probably people don't really

00:02:09.950 --> 00:02:12.660
think of us as doing this is camping.

00:02:12.660 --> 00:02:13.870
- [Kelly] Why would we
not think about that?

00:02:13.870 --> 00:02:15.830
- I don't know, because we live here,

00:02:15.830 --> 00:02:17.360
and I think of people who--
- [Kelly] It's a great place

00:02:17.360 --> 00:02:18.590
to camp!
- I know.

00:02:18.590 --> 00:02:19.780
Yeah, there's tons of places

00:02:19.780 --> 00:02:21.200
in California to camp.

00:02:21.200 --> 00:02:23.370
But we have a little Airstream trailer--

00:02:23.370 --> 00:02:25.190
- [Kelly] I love Airstream.
- And we just hook it up

00:02:25.190 --> 00:02:27.050
to the back (laughs) of the truck,

00:02:27.050 --> 00:02:28.580
and we take it places.

00:02:28.580 --> 00:02:29.413
And it's kinda cool

00:02:29.413 --> 00:02:31.050
because sometimes we'll
come into a campground,

00:02:31.050 --> 00:02:33.680
and it's night, so
people don't really know.

00:02:33.680 --> 00:02:35.270
People are usually asleep by then,

00:02:35.270 --> 00:02:37.778
so we just come in, make
a hell of a lot of noise.

00:02:37.778 --> 00:02:39.540
(laughing)

00:02:39.540 --> 00:02:40.373
- Can you imagine--
- And we're, "Sorry, sorry!"

00:02:40.373 --> 00:02:41.660
- The people, though, the next day,

00:02:41.660 --> 00:02:43.900
and they wake up, they go out,
they're making their eggs,

00:02:43.900 --> 00:02:45.647
and they're like, "What?"

00:02:46.680 --> 00:02:47.817
They're looking over, and they're like,

00:02:47.817 --> 00:02:49.950
"This just got amazing,

00:02:49.950 --> 00:02:52.097
this trip!"
(laughing)

